================================================================================

The anti-slop plugin ships term lists that incorporate material from the
following sources. Each is credited below with its own licence.

Individual words and short phrases are not protectable on their own. A curated
collection of them can be — as a compilation, and in the EU also under the sui
generis database right (Directive 96/9/EC). The two cases below follow
from that distinction: where a whole collection is carried over, its licence
travels with it; where only individual terms were drawn from a source, the credit
records provenance.

Read this together with LICENSE. The repository as a whole is Apache-2.0, and
that covers plugins/anti-slop/skills/anti-slop/data/ as well.

slop-list.json is an Apache-2.0 collection redistributed unmodified, so its
licence terms travel with it. Neither Apache-2.0 upstream below ships a NOTICE
file of its own (checked), so Apache-2.0 section 4(d) adds nothing further to
carry forward.

From the two CC BY-SA 4.0 Wikipedia pages, only individual terms and a category
structure were drawn. The terms are not protectable on their own; the structure
is an idea rather than an expression. Neither yields adapted material in the
sense of CC BY-SA 4.0 section 1(a), so the ShareAlike condition does not attach
and no part of data/ is licensed under CC BY-SA. The credit below records where
the terms came from, not a licence obligation being discharged.

